in indonesian waters off the coast of  the island of sumatra these fishermen  are trying to do whatever they can to  help people in desperate need this  vessel carrying more than 100 rohingya  refugees was spotted near aceh province  on sunday local fishermen said they felt  sorry for those on board particularly  the young children  according to our radio communication  they are weak someone died some are sick  including many children and the elderly  are getting weaker according to our  information they've been at sea for 28  days  [Music]  fishermen told al jazeera they have been  providing food medicine and water to  those on board but have not been allowed  to help them get to shore  local authorities in aceh say they will  assist the rohingya and give them  essential supplies but won't allow them  into the country  the job of the navy is to secure our  border  the rohingya are not indonesian citizens  we cannot bring them in even as refugees  this is in line with government policy  police say the rohingya were trying to  reach malaysia  thousands of rohingya have fled myanmar  to neighbouring bangladesh in recent  years escaping a military crackdown that  the un says amounts to genocide facing  poverty in crowded refugee camps many  try to reach safety in southeast asian  countries  last year a group of rohingya refugees  was saved by fishermen and brought to  shore after their boat broke down near  aceh but some fishermen told al jazeera  they were afraid of repercussions if  they attempted a rescue this time  indonesia is not a signatory to the u.n  convention on refugees and is seen as a  transit country for refugees making  dangerous journeys at sea  still human rights groups say indonesia  must do more as a regional power in  southeast asia  to push the boat back  is is a violations of international  obligations of indonesia i think  international law clearly imposes  obligations on states including  indonesia to protect human rights of  refugees arriving on their shores  the indonesian government has not  responded to al jazeera's requests for  comment and after so many days at sea  these desperate people still face an  uncertain future jessica washington al  jazeera jakarta
 

